Using the funcstack to preserve dynamic scope.

You can see how I wanted to shoehorn a new version of `show` into `exemplar`
for the purpose of using `exemplar` when we don't want to show each command as
it is run, and instead we want to show the command only on an exception.

We managed to avoid a switch by checking to see that we were both immediately
called by `tryable` and that the definition of `tryable` is in the same
directory as `exemplar`. Could this be a pattern that could be used in lieu of
command line switches? Not that I'm eager to so this, but could we have
`heredoc`, `quotedoc` and `printfdoc` wrapping `heredoc`? (This would blow up
our implementations of `abend`, `warn` and others, but it's an example.)

This test could be expressed in a case statement.

```
case $funcstack[2]:${functions_source[$funcstack[2]]:h} in
    (quotedoc:${functions_source[heredoc]:h})
        # quoted
        ;;
    (printfdoc:${functions_source[heredoc]:h})
        # printf
        ;;
    (*)
        # literal
        ;;
esac
```

This ought to be documented to explain `tryable` and to ensure that it does
not get lost. It will make sense if there are functions that only ever take
a single switch. The `heredoc`, `quotedoc`, and `printfdoc` are temping, but
they would mean doing soemting `abend`, `abendquote`, `abendf`, etc.
